From michal@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca Thu Mar  6 19:36:03 1997
Message-ID: <199703070033.RAA06078@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca>
Date: Thu, 6 Mar 1997 17:33:09 -0700 (MST)
From: Michal Jaegermann <michal@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca>
Subject: Re: Lock discs
In-Reply-To: <9703068576.AA857681600@ccgate.gtech.com>

> 
> 
>      Is there a way to make absolutely sure that the disc where my root 
>      partition is cannot be written to when I run a particular program 

The only "absolute way" is to turn the disk off.  If your program
writes to a raw SCSI device, without paying any attention to a file
system, it may write there whatever it pleases - commands to reformat
your drive including.

If it is better behaved then remounting your disk read-only should
be good enough.  Use '-n' option on mounts which follow as you will
be not able to update /etc/mtab anymore.  Also your editors will likely
fail as they may try to create some files in /tmp

>      Cdwrite has wiped it out twice and I will not run it again until I can 
>      feel safe.

Do you have to run it as a root???  Making output device
readable/writable to 'cdwriter', group 'cdwriter', with corresponding
permissions on a stuff you want to write, should make it safer.
At least you will get failed writes if you will try to send a SCSI
garbage to your main device (assuming that permissions there are sane)
instead of a trashed disk.

  Michal

